So we're all caught up and I have a great email question to answer this week,

85
00:06:03,500 --> 00:06:05,500
So let's get started. Shall we

86
00:06:33,500 --> 00:06:38,500
All time boy scout? You're my favorite All time boy scout.

87
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:42,500
He's my favorite All time boy scout.

88
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:47,500
Write me a letter, Send it by name

89
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:50,500
Email. that is folks.

90
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:54,500
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

91
00:06:54,500 --> 00:07:07,500
I received this email recently from a relatively new scout master who said:

92
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:13,500
I have a scout who's only interested in participating as long as it fulfills a requirement.

93
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:15,500
He's pretty open about this

94
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:19,500
And I've heard him remark in the presence of other scouts that he doesn't quote

95
00:07:19,500 --> 00:07:24,500
need to go on this hike or quote need to do this service project.

96
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:31,500
And I've heard him say: well, I'm only here to be credited for the camping night on camping trips. on the most recent camping trip

97
00:07:31,500 --> 00:07:35,500
He decided to leave before the rest of the troop was packed up and ready to head home.

98
00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:40,500
He asked an assistant scout master who misunderstood what was being asked and let him go.

99
00:07:40,500 --> 00:07:43,500
If he had asked me, I would have told him that he needed to stay.

100
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:48,500
I'd like to sit down and work with this scout on scout spirit issues.

101
00:07:48,500 --> 00:07:51,500
Can you give me a sense of the conversation I need to have with him?

102
00:07:51,500 --> 00:07:54,500
This is a scout who needs what we have to offer

103
00:07:54,500 --> 00:07:58,500
And I sense he can make some real progress and I want to work with him without pushing him away.

104
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:03,500
Well, this is a great question and I want to answer it in a couple of different ways.

105
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:09,500
First of all, we'll deal with the specific question that's been posed and then talk about, in a more general way,

106
00:08:09,500 --> 00:08:13,500
these sorts of problems, because these sorts of problems

107
00:08:13,500 --> 00:08:18,500
they're not, you know, disruptive, dangerous, inappropriate.

108
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:20,500
They're just kind of nettlesome-

109
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:26,500
And I love the word nettlesome, because if you've ever been out in the woods and you've run across a bunch of nettles

110
00:08:26,500 --> 00:08:28,500
and gotten stung, you know what I'm talking about.

111
00:08:28,500 --> 00:08:33,500
It's a pain and it lasts for a little while and it's really aggravating.

112
00:08:33,500 --> 00:08:34,500
It's not dangerous,

113
00:08:34,500 --> 00:08:40,500
It's just. why would you want to have to deal with something like this?

114
00:08:40,500 --> 00:08:45,500
Now, everybody thinks it's a scout master's responsibility to fix all these nettlesome problems,

115
00:08:45,500 --> 00:08:47,500
but it isn't, at least not directly.

116
00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:53,500
You can very effectively point out that the scouts themselves can fix these problems.

117
00:08:53,500 --> 00:09:00,500
They're empowered to shape their behavior and their attitudes, of course, by applying the scout oath and law.

118
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:04,500
So how do we deal with this specific question?

119
00:09:04,500 --> 00:09:09,500
Well, the first rule of thumb is always to pay attention to behavior you want to see

120
00:09:09,500 --> 00:09:12,500
and don't react to behavior you don't want to see,

121
00:09:12,500 --> 00:09:17,500
Of course, unless there are issues of safety or propriety involved.

122
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:22,500
Somebody has a bad attitude or kind of a cavalier idea of what they're doing in scouts.

123
00:09:22,500 --> 00:09:26,500
Don't pay attention to it. and I'll describe exactly what I mean

124
00:09:26,500 --> 00:09:29,500
In this particular instance. if I was in your shoes,

125
00:09:29,500 --> 00:09:34,500
I would take the opportunity presented by my next scout master's minute

126
00:09:34,500 --> 00:09:38,500
and I would talk about the kind of shared responsibilities we have when we go camping

127
00:09:38,500 --> 00:09:44,500
And I would talk about specific points of the scout oath and law in that context.

128
00:09:44,500 --> 00:09:47,500
And you know that's easy to figure out, right?

129
00:09:47,500 --> 00:09:49,500
A scout is helpful, a scout is friendly.

130
00:09:49,500 --> 00:09:52,500
We help other people at all times, that sort of thing.

131
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:58,500
And then I would mention the name of a scout who was particularly helpful

132
00:09:58,500 --> 00:10:03,500
in the same circumstances when the troop was packing up to leave on that Sunday morning

133
00:10:03,500 --> 00:10:08,500
This weekend. when we were packing up, I saw John working pretty hard

134
00:10:08,500 --> 00:10:11,500
and helping everybody out, and I know he's a really busy guy.

135
00:10:11,500 --> 00:10:13,500
He probably had plenty of other things to do that day,

136
00:10:13,500 --> 00:10:16,500
But, John, I just want to say thank you for staying with the troop

137
00:10:16,500 --> 00:10:20,500
and doing your part, because that's a great example of the scout oath and law.

138
00:10:20,500 --> 00:10:24,500
Now, maybe you saw something like that and maybe you didn't.

139
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:27,500
A little creative fibbing is permissible,

140
00:10:27,500 --> 00:10:33,500
But the idea is to be relentlessly positive.

141
00:10:33,500 --> 00:10:39,500
Don't describe bad behavior and then tell the scouts that that's not what you want to see.

142
00:10:39,500 --> 00:10:45,500
Don't call out the offending nettlesome scout in front of the others

143
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:47,500
and try and make an example of him.

144
00:10:47,500 --> 00:10:49,500
Be relentlessly positive.

145
00:10:49,500 --> 00:10:54,500
Your scouts will absolutely understand what's going on there,

146
00:10:54,500 --> 00:10:58,500
As will the scout who decided he could go home early.

147
00:10:58,500 --> 00:11:02,500
And while we're talking about this specific thing, you also need to realize

148
00:11:02,500 --> 00:11:06,500
this scout, or any other scout, has only to fulfill the requirements.

149
00:11:06,500 --> 00:11:09,500
They can do that with a terribly bad attitude

150
00:11:09,500 --> 00:11:12,500
If they choose to. they can be very cavalier and cynical about it

151
00:11:12,500 --> 00:11:15,500
If they choose to. you won't like that.

152
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:17,500
Requirements are written down.

153
00:11:17,500 --> 00:11:20,500
There's not a whole lot of argument whether they've been fulfilled or not.

154
00:11:20,500 --> 00:11:23,500
You just don't like their attitude and it really bugs you

155
00:11:23,500 --> 00:11:25,500
And that's a nettlesome problem.

156
00:11:25,500 --> 00:11:27,500
But you don't need to solve it.

157
00:11:27,500 --> 00:11:31,500
You just need to create an atmosphere in which these kind of nettlesome problems are very small.

158
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:33,500
And when it comes to advancement, come on,

159
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:40,500
Advancement requirements are such a small part of the whole picture of what we want to do for our scouts

160
00:11:40,500 --> 00:11:42,500
And really fulfilling them is easy.

161
00:11:42,500 --> 00:11:46,500
It's not that big a deal if you're in an active camping troop and patrol.

162
00:11:46,500 --> 00:11:49,500
If you let things like this get to you as a scouter,

163
00:11:49,500 --> 00:11:52,500
you'll find you're going to spend all your time worrying about them.

164
00:11:52,500 --> 00:11:57,500
And I understand that because I have been in that place and I still get in that place once in a while.

165
00:11:57,500 --> 00:12:00,500
I have to remind myself of these things.

166
00:12:00,500 --> 00:12:02,500
So what about the question of scout spirit?

167
00:12:02,500 --> 00:12:06,500
Because you want to sit down and talk to this particular scout about scout spirit.

168
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:08,500
So here's rule of thumb number two:

169
00:12:08,500 --> 00:12:15,500
Don't ever put yourself in the role of judging whether or not a scout has scout spirit

170
00:12:15,500 --> 00:12:19,500
or has fulfilled any advancement requirement.

171
00:12:19,500 --> 00:12:21,500
Don't put yourself in that spot

172
00:12:21,500 --> 00:12:25,500
When you're asked to sign any requirement, any requirement at all.

173
00:12:25,500 --> 00:12:31,500
ask the scout to judge himself based on his own inner standard,

174
00:12:31,500 --> 00:12:35,500
And you will find most all of the time you will agree with their judgment.

175
00:12:35,500 --> 00:12:40,500
In the few cases that you don't, you'll find the scouts being too hard on themselves.

176
00:12:40,500 --> 00:12:43,500
In the one or two cases a year where you disagree.

177
00:12:43,500 --> 00:12:48,500
all you have to do is hold up a mirror by relating what you have observed

178
00:12:48,500 --> 00:12:52,500
and asking: do you think, based on what I've just said,

179
00:12:52,500 --> 00:12:54,500
you have actually fulfilled this requirement?

180
00:12:54,500 --> 00:12:59,500
So let's go step by step on exactly how this would work in this particular case.

181
00:12:59,500 --> 00:13:03,500
Let's say this: this scouts attitude never changes and you're troubled by it.

182
00:13:03,500 --> 00:13:08,500
and they want to have you sign off the requirement about being active and showing scout spirit.

183
00:13:08,500 --> 00:13:13,500
You ask them to say: well, I want to know if you think you fulfilled that requirement.

184
00:13:13,500 --> 00:13:17,500
The scout will say something like I'm not sure or yes, I have fulfilled the requirement,

185
00:13:17,500 --> 00:13:21,500
And then you say: okay, so tell me how you arrive at that conclusion,

186
00:13:21,500 --> 00:13:26,500
Tell me how you judge that you have fulfilled that requirement.

187
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:31,500
The first time you go through this with a scout, they're going to be a little befuddled

188
00:13:31,500 --> 00:13:33,500
because people don't ask this question of them.

189
00:13:33,500 --> 00:13:37,500
People don't ask them what they're thinking and what their judgment of the situation is.

190
00:13:37,500 --> 00:13:42,500
Usually they just make a judgment for them and either sign or not sign a requirement

191
00:13:42,500 --> 00:13:45,500
or an assignment at school or something like that.

192
00:13:45,500 --> 00:13:49,500
So you're asking them to go through a process they might not be very familiar with.

193
00:13:49,500 --> 00:13:52,500
Give them a little time ask some questions.

194
00:13:52,500 --> 00:13:54,500
Well, what do you think this requirement means?

195
00:13:54,500 --> 00:13:55,500
What is scout spirit anyway?

196
00:13:55,500 --> 00:13:56,500
What does that mean?

197
00:13:56,500 --> 00:14:01,500
And you'll have a lovely discussion where you can talk back and forth about the way that this works.

198
00:14:01,500 --> 00:14:07,500
and what you're doing as this discussion unfolds is you're getting a picture of their inner standard,

199
00:14:07,500 --> 00:14:11,500
because our aim is the development of character, and character is having some standards

200
00:14:11,500 --> 00:14:13,500
and holding yourself to those standards.

201
00:14:13,500 --> 00:14:19,500
You'll get a real good idea of exactly where the scout is in that phase of his development as a person.

202
00:14:19,500 --> 00:14:26,500
So you give this a shot. see if I'm not right, because most of the time, in the vast majority of cases,

203
00:14:26,500 --> 00:14:31,500
you're going to agree with the judgment that the scout has made about their own performance.

204
00:14:31,500 --> 00:14:38,500
or, in a smaller number of cases, you're going to find that the scout is being too difficult on themselves.

205
00:14:38,500 --> 00:14:45,500
or, in the very small number of cases, you're just going to absolutely disagree with their judgment.

206
00:14:45,500 --> 00:14:50,500
So let's say that the scouting question- this one who's had this nettlesome attitude- says,

207
00:14:50,500 --> 00:14:56,500
yes, I fulfilled that requirement. and you have a discussion with them about exactly what fulfilling that requirement means

208
00:14:56,500 --> 00:15:02,500
and you're not really getting anywhere. you say, okay. so look, I've heard you say this before.

209
00:15:02,500 --> 00:15:04,500
I've seen you do this.

210
00:15:04,500 --> 00:15:06,500
So we're holding up a mirror,

211
00:15:06,500 --> 00:15:12,500
We're just saying these are the things that you have said or done that are germane to this requirement.

212
00:15:12,500 --> 00:15:17,500
So do you think somebody who says or does those sort of things has fulfilled this requirement?

213
00:15:17,500 --> 00:15:20,500
And maybe you'll get somewhere and maybe you won't.

214
00:15:20,500 --> 00:15:25,500
And it's perfectly acceptable to say: well, look, you know, we've discussed this,

215
00:15:25,500 --> 00:15:26,500
We disagree about it.

216
00:15:26,500 --> 00:15:28,500
What kind of a plan can we make?

217
00:15:28,500 --> 00:15:35,500
So we're both satisfied, when we pick this issue up again, that you've satisfied this requirement.

218
00:15:35,500 --> 00:15:41,500
So you start having discussions like that with scouts and you get a very good understanding of who they are,

219
00:15:41,500 --> 00:15:47,500
where they are developmentally, what they have understood or not understood about scouting,

220
00:15:47,500 --> 00:15:54,500
And it opens the door to a continuing relationship where you can help them figure out how to develop their character.

221
00:15:54,500 --> 00:16:00,500
So back to thinking about nettle sum problems, and remember what we've defined nettle sum, as

222
00:16:00,500 --> 00:16:08,500
These are things that are not safety problems or behavior discipline problems that rise to the standard of needing absolute correction.

223
00:16:08,500 --> 00:16:12,500
These are things that just kind of bug you,

224
00:16:12,500 --> 00:16:18,500
And they could be a long list of things that bug you about the attitude or the actions or the speech of your scouts.

225
00:16:18,500 --> 00:16:20,500
A lot of things can bug you.

226
00:16:20,500 --> 00:16:32,500
So when it comes to making a judgment call about scout spirit or being active or any one of another dozen things as effects evaluating a scout towards fulfilling a requirement,

227
00:16:32,500 --> 00:16:39,500
one dodge a lot of scouts end up taking is kind of lawyering things around and making rules.

228
00:16:39,500 --> 00:16:46,500
So in this situation, you know it might fall out that after you think about this for a while you decide: well, I'll just make a rule.

229
00:16:46,500 --> 00:16:48,500
Nobody leaves the campsite early.

230
00:16:48,500 --> 00:16:51,500
There we go. The rule fixes it.

231
00:16:51,500 --> 00:16:54,500
But but the rule does not fix anything.

232
00:16:54,500 --> 00:17:01,500
It just makes everything worse, because rules are powerless to change the underlying attitudes that people have.

233
00:17:01,500 --> 00:17:05,500
The only thing you can do with rules is compel people to be compliant.

234
00:17:05,500 --> 00:17:07,500
You can't make them nicer people.

235
00:17:07,500 --> 00:17:10,500
You don't change the way that they look at the world.

236
00:17:10,500 --> 00:17:13,500
All you can do is force their compliance.

237
00:17:13,500 --> 00:17:24,500
And then, once you've established a rule, you put yourself into the role of policing the rule and judging the consequences and meeting out the consequences.

238
00:17:24,500 --> 00:17:31,500
And once you go down that road it takes over the way that you think about scouts, the way that you think about scouting,

239
00:17:31,500 --> 00:17:40,500
and you end up squandering your energy on what is a useless diversion from what scouting is actually aimed at:

240
00:17:40,500 --> 00:17:46,500
Rules. take away your most effective tool, and that's discussion and discretion.

241
00:17:46,500 --> 00:17:52,500
We have to realize we're working with young people at the most volatile and changeable time of their lives

242
00:17:52,500 --> 00:17:59,500
and we want all possible latitude to be able to work with individual challenges.

243
00:17:59,500 --> 00:18:05,500
And for every rule that you create, you take away a big chunk of the real estate there

244
00:18:05,500 --> 00:18:12,500
where you can work with an individual scout on an individual thing and come up with a resolution to it.

245
00:18:12,500 --> 00:18:21,500
Now look, I know that there are plenty of troops and scouts out there who have long lists of rules and regulations and policies and procedures

246
00:18:21,500 --> 00:18:25,500
and they get an entire troop of very compliant scouts.

247
00:18:25,500 --> 00:18:31,500
You can't force young people to have scout spirit by applying a list of rules.

248
00:18:31,500 --> 00:18:39,500
You have to encourage the growth of that scout spirit by getting them to think about their actions

249
00:18:39,500 --> 00:18:43,500
and their attitudes in the light of the scout oath and law.

250
00:18:43,500 --> 00:18:45,500
It really is just that simple,

251
00:18:45,500 --> 00:18:52,500
And I understand, because whether or not I have very specific, strong standards for my own attitudes and conduct

252
00:18:52,500 --> 00:18:57,500
and I think that they should be universally applied has got nothing to do with anything at all,

253
00:18:57,500 --> 00:19:02,500
because what you want your scouts to do is to understand the scout oath and law

254
00:19:02,500 --> 00:19:07,500
and apply them in their own lives to the best of their ability,

255
00:19:07,500 --> 00:19:15,500
And once you start thinking that way and you understand that the door opens up to being able to really help young people.

256
00:19:15,500 --> 00:19:23,500
So just quickly, by way of review. scoutmasters and scouters are not supposed to resolve every single problem that happens.

257
00:19:23,500 --> 00:19:32,500
Sometimes. scouts will say or do things or act in certain ways or maintain attitudes that you find disturbing or nettlesome.

258
00:19:32,500 --> 00:19:34,500
They're not discipline problems,

259
00:19:34,500 --> 00:19:36,500
They're not dangerous,

260
00:19:36,500 --> 00:19:38,500
They're not just wholly inappropriate,

261
00:19:38,500 --> 00:19:41,500
They're just a little off key and it kind of drives you a little crazy.

262
00:19:41,500 --> 00:19:47,500
Most of the time these will have their origin in something to do with advancement.

263
00:19:47,500 --> 00:19:48,500
So take my advice:

264
00:19:48,500 --> 00:19:50,500
Don't pay any attention to those things.

265
00:19:50,500 --> 00:19:52,500
Pay attention to the behavior you want to see,

266
00:19:52,500 --> 00:19:58,500
Praise the behavior you want to see publicly and often- and the scouts begin to get the idea-

267
00:19:58,500 --> 00:20:05,500
Don't substitute your judgment for the judgment of the scout when it comes to evaluating an advancement requirement.

268
00:20:05,500 --> 00:20:10,500
Call upon the inner standard that they are developing and discuss it with them.

269
00:20:10,500 --> 00:20:16,500
It's during those discussions that you can really drill down into the scout oath and law

270
00:20:16,500 --> 00:20:20,500
and develop an understanding of what your scouts think about it

271
00:20:20,500 --> 00:20:23,500
and how they go about applying it.

272
00:20:23,500 --> 00:20:28,500
And that is a great place to put your energy, because that is our aim:

273
00:20:28,500 --> 00:20:32,500
to develop character by the application of the patrol method.

274
00:20:32,500 --> 00:20:34,500
So give that a try and get back to me.

275
00:20:34,500 --> 00:20:35,500
I want to learn.

276
00:20:35,500 --> 00:20:38,500
